('127.400.d.4.A',3,'Text justification determines how text is aligned relative to its:','["plot scale", "insertion point", "units", "layer"]'::jsonb,1,'Justification options such as left, center, right.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.A',4,'Multi-line text differs from single-line text because it can wrap within a:','["dimension", "layer name", "defined text box width", "block"]'::jsonb,2,'Multi-line text uses a bounding box.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.B',3,'A multi-leader is a note with an arrow and a line pointing to a:','["printer", "layer name", "different file", "feature on the drawing"]'::jsonb,3,'Leaders connect notes to features.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.B',4,'One multi-leader can point to several features if the drafter adds extra:','["leader lines", "units", "plot styles", "layers"]'::jsonb,0,'Multi-leaders can have several leaders.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.C',3,'Editing a dimension''s text so that it shows a tolerance is done with the dimension:','["zoom tool", "edit tools or properties", "layer manager", "print settings"]'::jsonb,1,'Dimensions are edited after creation.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.C',4,'If a dimension text is overridden by typing a number, the dimension will no longer:','["print", "belong to a layer", "update automatically with the geometry", "display"]'::jsonb,2,'Overrides break associativity.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.D',3,'To make all dimensions on a drawing use a smaller arrow, a drafter should change the:','["layer lock", "plot area", "units", "dimension style"]'::jsonb,3,'Styles govern all dimensions that use them.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.D',4,'Different dimension styles may be needed when one drawing contains both:','["metric and inch dimensions", "layers and blocks", "text and tables", "two colors"]'::jsonb,0,'Units may need separate styles.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.E',3,'Crosshatching on a section view represents the:','["title block", "cut material", "hidden lines", "dimension"]'::jsonb,1,'Hatch patterns show cut surfaces.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.E',4,'Different hatch patterns on an assembly section are used to show:','["different layers", "different scales", "different materials or parts", "different units"]'::jsonb,2,'Patterns distinguish materials.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.F',3,'Isolating an object temporarily hides:','["the title block only", "the selected object only", "nothing", "all other objects"]'::jsonb,3,'Isolate shows only selected items.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.F',4,'Hiding objects that clutter a view is helpful when a drafter needs to:','["work on a specific part of a crowded drawing", "change units", "delete the drawing", "close the file"]'::jsonb,0,'Hiding reduces visual clutter.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.G',3,'A window selection selects only the objects that are:','["partly inside", "completely inside the window", "on other layers", "outside the window"]'::jsonb,1,'Window requires full enclosure.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.G',4,'A crossing selection selects objects that are:','["dimension-only", "entirely outside", "inside or touching the selection rectangle", "hidden only"]'::jsonb,2,'Crossing selects touched objects.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.H',3,'A rectangular array copies an object into rows and:','["layers", "blocks", "scales", "columns"]'::jsonb,3,'Rectangular arrays use rows and columns.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.H',4,'A polar array of 6 equally spaced copies around a full circle places them how many degrees apart?','["60", "30", "90", "6"]'::jsonb,0,'360 / 6 = 60.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.I',3,'The reference option of the rotate command lets a drafter rotate an object to match an existing:','["layer", "angle", "style", "scale"]'::jsonb,1,'Reference rotation aligns to existing geometry.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.I',4,'An object currently at 30 degrees must end up at 90 degrees. Using a reference, it rotates:','["90 degrees", "30 degrees", "60 degrees", "120 degrees"]'::jsonb,2,'90 minus 30.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.J',3,'A balanced layout with consistent alignment on a drawing sheet shows good use of:','["hidden layers", "blank space only", "random placement", "design and organizational principles"]'::jsonb,3,'Order and balance help communication.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.J',4,'Using more line weight for important outlines and lighter weight for details creates:','["visual hierarchy", "hidden lines", "confusion", "errors in scale"]'::jsonb,0,'Hierarchy guides the reader.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.K',3,'Setting a layer''s color, linetype, and lineweight means all objects on that layer will:','["move", "follow those properties by layer", "lose their shape", "be deleted"]'::jsonb,1,'ByLayer properties are controlled centrally.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.K',4,'Locking a layer prevents its objects from being:','["viewed", "counted", "accidentally edited", "plotted"]'::jsonb,2,'Locked layers are visible but protected.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.L',3,'Page setup stores the plotter, paper size, and plot:','["user passwords", "file owners", "fonts only", "scale and area"]'::jsonb,3,'Page setup saves plot settings.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.L',4,'Saving a named page setup helps a drafter to:','["reuse the same plot settings on many layouts", "skip checking", "change units", "delete layers"]'::jsonb,0,'Named setups save time.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.M',3,'Reusable content such as a bolt symbol can be stored as a:','["viewport only", "block", "layer", "grid"]'::jsonb,1,'Blocks make reusable symbols.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.M',4,'A tool palette can store blocks so that they can be:','["hidden permanently", "printed only", "dragged into a drawing quickly", "deleted automatically"]'::jsonb,2,'Palettes provide quick access.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.N',3,'The Standard Alphabet of Lines defines the line types used for:','["email", "tax forms", "music", "object, hidden, center, and other lines on drawings"]'::jsonb,3,'It standardizes line meaning.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.N',4,'Under the Alphabet of Lines, a center line is drawn with:','["long and short dashes alternating", "dotted line", "thick solid line", "phantom only"]'::jsonb,0,'Center lines use long-short dashes.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.O',3,'A gradient fill is an area filled with a smooth transition between:','["two units", "two colors", "two layers", "two text styles"]'::jsonb,1,'Gradients blend colors.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.O',4,'A solid fill is often used in a drawing to emphasize:','["the units", "the title block border", "a cut or important area", "every line"]'::jsonb,2,'Fills highlight areas.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.P',3,'The angle of a hatch pattern can be changed to avoid lines that are:','["on another layer", "longer", "in the title block", "parallel to an adjacent object''s edge"]'::jsonb,3,'Angle changes improve clarity.','original'),
('127.400.d.4.P',4,'Increasing a hatch pattern''s scale makes the pattern spacing:','["wider", "invisible", "darker only", "tighter"]'::jsonb,0,'Larger scale means wider spacing.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.A',3,'Translating a hand sketch into CADD begins with setting up the file with the correct:','["colors only", "units and layers", "printer name", "wallpaper"]'::jsonb,1,'Setup precedes drawing.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.A',4,'When a hand sketch has no exact dimensions, the drafter should:','["guess all sizes", "draw in color only", "confirm dimensions with the designer before drawing", "skip the part"]'::jsonb,2,'Clarify before drawing.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.B',3,'A projected mechanical drawing aligns the front, top, and side views using:','["fonts", "layer names", "text styles", "projection lines"]'::jsonb,3,'Views are aligned by projection.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.B',4,'The width of the top view must match the width of the front view because both views show the same:','["dimension of the object", "color", "layer", "file size"]'::jsonb,0,'Shared dimensions must agree.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.C',3,'Attaching a gear drawing as an external reference keeps the main drawing:','["larger always", "smaller and updated when the gear changes", "fixed forever", "locked"]'::jsonb,1,'Xrefs reduce file size and update links.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.C',4,'If an external reference file is moved to a different folder, the main drawing may show:','["a title block", "a new layer", "a missing reference warning", "a larger plot"]'::jsonb,2,'Path changes break references.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.D',3,'In a parametric model, changing a dimension value causes the model to:','["lose its parts", "become fixed", "be deleted", "update automatically"]'::jsonb,3,'Parametric designs follow parameters.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.D',4,'Constraints in a parametric sketch, such as tangent or parallel, ensure the model:','["keeps its geometric relationships when edited", "cannot be saved", "loses its shape", "has no dimensions"]'::jsonb,0,'Constraints preserve design intent.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.E',3,'An animated exploded assembly helps viewers understand:','["the price of parts", "the order in which parts go together", "the color palette", "the sheet size"]'::jsonb,1,'Animation shows assembly sequence.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.E',4,'In a complex assembly of hundreds of parts, organizing the parts into subassemblies makes the model easier to:','["hide", "delete", "manage and edit", "print only"]'::jsonb,2,'Subassemblies simplify structure.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.F',3,'A wireframe model represents an object using only its:','["faces", "volume", "mass", "edges"]'::jsonb,3,'Wireframes have edges only.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.F',4,'Of the three 3D model types, only a solid model contains information about the object''s:','["volume and mass properties", "scale", "layers", "colors"]'::jsonb,0,'Solids carry mass properties.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.G',3,'A composite drawing is made by combining parts from several drawings into:','["no drawing", "one drawing", "several printers", "a table"]'::jsonb,1,'Composite drawings assemble sources.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.G',4,'Before combining drawings, the drafter should make sure the drawings use the same:','["owners", "fonts only", "units and scale", "dates"]'::jsonb,2,'Matching units prevent errors.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.H',3,'A drafter who can explain why a section view was chosen is showing the ability to:','["skip reviews", "avoid standards", "hide layers", "justify drawing methods"]'::jsonb,3,'Justification explains choices.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.H',4,'Using an auxiliary view for a slanted surface is justified because it shows that surface in its:','["true size and shape", "smallest form", "thickest form", "hidden form"]'::jsonb,0,'Auxiliary views show true size of inclined faces.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.I',3,'The lines, arcs, and circles drawn in a floor plan or a gear drawing are made on:','["one block", "separate layers by object type", "a single color", "a blank page"]'::jsonb,1,'Layering organizes objects.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.I',4,'Drawing a bolt circle with six holes starts by drawing a:','["spline", "table", "center circle and one hole, then arraying it", "dimension"]'::jsonb,2,'A polar array creates the pattern.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.J',3,'Notes in a title block are often placed using middle-center justification so that the text is:','["pushed out of the box", "deleted", "stretched", "centered in its box"]'::jsonb,3,'Justification aligns text in a space.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.J',4,'To make a general note wrap neatly inside a narrow column on a sheet, use:','["multi-line text with a set width", "a hatch", "a block attribute", "a leader"]'::jsonb,0,'MTEXT wraps by width.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.K',3,'A multi-leader placed on a part with a callout number helps readers locate an:','["file", "item in the parts list", "layer", "plot style"]'::jsonb,1,'Callouts tie to the list.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.K',4,'Aligning several multi-leaders so that their text lines up makes a mechanical assembly drawing look:','["unscaled", "wrong", "neat and professional", "incomplete"]'::jsonb,2,'Alignment tools improve appearance.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
insert into public.question_bank (teks_code, teks_item_id, subject, grade_num, course, difficulty, qtype, prompt, choices, answer_idx, explanation, source, status, gen_batch)
select distinct on (v.prompt) v.code, i.id, d.subject,
       case when i.grade_level = 'K' then 0 when i.grade_level ~ '^[0-9]+' then substring(i.grade_level from '^[0-9]+')::smallint end,
       case when i.grade_level is null then i.grade_course_title end,
       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_60_v1'
from (values
('127.400.d.5.L',3,'To add a tolerance of plus or minus 0.005 to an existing dimension, edit the dimension''s:','["viewport", "layer lock", "hatch", "tolerance setting in its style or properties"]'::jsonb,3,'Tolerances are set in style or properties.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.L',4,'Overriding a single dimension in a drawing changes only that dimension, while editing the style changes:','["all dimensions that use the style", "none", "only the first", "only the last"]'::jsonb,0,'Style edits apply broadly.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.M',3,'Hiding the dimension layer temporarily lets a drafter inspect the:','["plot style", "geometry without clutter", "file name", "title block"]'::jsonb,1,'Hiding layers aids inspection.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.M',4,'Unisolating objects after working on one part restores:','["the previous file", "the deleted objects", "the display of all hidden objects", "a blank screen"]'::jsonb,2,'Unisolate returns the view.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.N',3,'The fence selection method selects objects that are:','["inside a window only", "hidden", "on other layers", "crossed by a line drawn through them"]'::jsonb,3,'A fence picks what it touches.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.N',4,'A selection set saved with a name or filter lets a drafter:','["reselect the same group of objects later", "print twice", "delete layers", "close the file"]'::jsonb,0,'Saved sets speed up repeated edits.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.O',3,'Using a consistent layout for the views, notes, and title block on every sheet shows the principle of:','["clutter", "organization and consistency", "contrast only", "randomness"]'::jsonb,1,'Consistency helps readers.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.O',4,'Leaving white space around views on a drawing sheet makes the sheet more:','["confusing", "crowded", "readable", "costly"]'::jsonb,2,'Space improves clarity.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.P',3,'Layer names such as DIM, HIDDEN, and CENTER help a team to:','["lose files", "hide the title", "slow down", "find and control objects by purpose"]'::jsonb,3,'Meaningful names help organization.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.P',4,'A layer standard shared by a design team ensures that drawings from different people:','["look and behave consistently", "differ", "fail to print", "lose data"]'::jsonb,0,'Standards promote consistency.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.Q',3,'The plot style table controls how colors on screen print as:','["layer names", "line weights and colors on paper", "units", "file types"]'::jsonb,1,'Plot styles map objects to output.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.Q',4,'A plot setup that plots only the window selected should be used when you want to print:','["a different file", "the layers", "a portion of the drawing", "a blank sheet"]'::jsonb,2,'Window plotting prints part of the drawing.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.R',3,'A full section view is created by passing a cutting plane through the:','["title block", "printer", "layer manager", "entire object"]'::jsonb,3,'Full sections cut completely through.','original'),
('127.400.d.5.R',4,'An auxiliary view is projected perpendicular to a(n):','["inclined surface", "title block", "text", "horizontal line only"]'::jsonb,0,'Auxiliary views show slanted faces in true size.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.A',3,'A schematic drawing shows a circuit using:','["solid models", "symbols and connecting lines", "floor plans", "sections"]'::jsonb,1,'Schematics use symbols.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.A',4,'A schematic differs from a wiring drawing because it shows how a circuit works rather than the:','["resistor values", "symbols", "physical routing of wires", "power source"]'::jsonb,2,'Schematics show function, not layout.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.B',3,'A printed circuit board assembly package typically includes a parts list and a drawing of where parts are:','["sold", "stored", "shipped", "placed on the board"]'::jsonb,3,'Assembly drawings locate components.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.B',4,'The silkscreen layer of a printed circuit board drawing is used to print:','["reference designators and outlines on the board", "power", "copper traces", "drill holes"]'::jsonb,0,'Silkscreen marks component labels.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.C',3,'A connection drawing shows how the external wires attach to a:','["viewport", "terminal or connector", "plot", "layer"]'::jsonb,1,'Connection drawings show terminations.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.C',4,'Terminal numbers on a connection drawing help a technician to:','["change the scale", "measure voltage", "connect each wire to the right point", "plot faster"]'::jsonb,2,'Numbers prevent miswiring.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.D',3,'An interconnection drawing shows the wiring between:','["layers", "fonts", "plot styles", "separate units or assemblies"]'::jsonb,3,'Interconnection links units.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.D',4,'On an interconnection drawing, cable designations tell the installer which cable runs between which:','["units", "scales", "fonts", "layers"]'::jsonb,0,'Designations identify cables.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.E',3,'A wiring drawing shows the path of wires and their:','["heights", "color codes or numbers", "prices", "paint"]'::jsonb,1,'Wires are identified by code.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.E',4,'A list accompanying a wiring drawing gives the ''from'' and ''to'' points of each:','["layer", "scale", "wire", "font"]'::jsonb,2,'Wire lists give endpoints.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.F',3,'A cable or harness drawing shows how wires are grouped and:','["painted", "priced", "hidden", "bundled, with lengths and connectors"]'::jsonb,3,'Harness drawings define assembly.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.F',4,'A harness board drawing is often made full size so that technicians can lay wires:','["directly on the drawing", "on the wall", "in a drawer", "in the trash"]'::jsonb,0,'Full-size boards guide assembly.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.G',3,'A component drawing of an electronic part gives its dimensions and its:','["owner", "lead or pin locations", "color only", "price only"]'::jsonb,1,'Component drawings locate pins.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.G',4,'A footprint drawing of a component used in circuit board design shows the:','["battery type", "time", "pad pattern where the part is soldered", "circuit voltage"]'::jsonb,2,'Footprints define pads.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.H',3,'A logic diagram uses symbols such as AND, OR, and NOT to show:','["paint colors", "soil types", "floor layouts", "how signals are processed"]'::jsonb,3,'Logic diagrams show gates.','original'),
('127.400.d.6.H',4,'An AND gate outputs a high signal only when:','["all of its inputs are high", "any input is high", "one input is low", "no input exists"]'::jsonb,0,'AND needs all inputs high.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.A',3,'A gear drawing includes data such as the number of teeth and the:','["supplier", "pitch diameter", "paint color", "price"]'::jsonb,1,'Gear data tables list teeth and pitch.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.A',4,'A spring drawing must give the wire diameter, the free length, and the:','["scale of the sheet", "owner", "number of coils", "date only"]'::jsonb,2,'Spring data defines the part.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.B',3,'A detail drawing provides all the information needed to make a single:','["contract", "building", "assembly line", "part"]'::jsonb,3,'Detail drawings describe one part.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.B',4,'In addition to the views and dimensions, a detail drawing includes the material and the:','["tolerances", "supplier''s address", "owner''s name", "budget"]'::jsonb,0,'Tolerances define acceptable variation.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.C',3,'A surface development is a drawing that shows a surface:','["colored", "laid out flat", "rotated", "hidden"]'::jsonb,1,'Developments flatten surfaces.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.C',4,'Sheet metal ductwork, such as a transition fitting, is made from a pattern created through:','["rendering", "hatching", "surface development", "plotting"]'::jsonb,2,'Developments produce patterns.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.D',3,'A weld symbol on a drawing tells the fabricator the type and the:','["owner", "layer", "color", "size of the weld"]'::jsonb,3,'Weld symbols carry size and type.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.D',4,'A weld symbol placed below the reference line indicates that the weld is on the:','["arrow side of the joint", "outside of the sheet", "other side", "hidden side"]'::jsonb,0,'Below the line is the arrow side.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.E',3,'A drawing of a bearing must include the bore diameter and the:','["customer", "outside diameter", "owner", "color"]'::jsonb,1,'Bearing drawings include the diameters.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.E',4,'The fit of a bearing on a shaft is controlled by giving the shaft and the bore:','["names", "colors", "tolerances", "symbols only"]'::jsonb,2,'Fits come from tolerances.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.F',3,'A casting drawing shows the shape of a part made by pouring metal into a:','["pipe", "press", "frame", "mold"]'::jsonb,3,'Casting uses molds.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.F',4,'Casting drawings include draft angles so that the pattern can be removed from the:','["mold", "drawer", "press", "floor"]'::jsonb,0,'Draft allows release.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.G',3,'A forging drawing shows the shape of a part made by shaping heated metal with:','["paint", "compressive force", "water", "light"]'::jsonb,1,'Forging uses force.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.G',4,'A forging drawing often shows the finished part outline inside the forging outline to show the amount of:','["labor", "paint", "material machined away", "packing"]'::jsonb,2,'The difference is stock for machining.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.H',3,'A tool drawing, such as a jig or fixture drawing, describes a device that helps to hold or guide a:','["owner", "budget", "paint", "workpiece"]'::jsonb,3,'Jigs and fixtures hold work.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.H',4,'A jig differs from a fixture because a jig also:','["guides the cutting tool", "has no parts", "costs less", "is always smaller"]'::jsonb,0,'Jigs guide tools.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.I',3,'A molding diagram for plastic parts shows the part shape, the parting line, and the:','["price", "gate location", "color of paper", "owner"]'::jsonb,1,'Gates feed plastic into the mold.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.I',4,'Uniform wall thickness in a molded plastic part helps to reduce:','["weight only", "price", "warping and sink marks", "color"]'::jsonb,2,'Uniform walls cool evenly.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.J',3,'A stamping drawing shows the shape of a part made by pressing sheet metal with a:','["roller", "saw", "brush", "die"]'::jsonb,3,'Stamping uses dies.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.J',4,'On a stamping drawing, the allowance is the length of material needed to form each:','["bend", "title", "hole", "layer"]'::jsonb,0,'Bend allowance accounts for bending.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.K',3,'Numerical-control drawings give dimensions from a:','["random point", "zero point (datum)", "moving point", "sign"]'::jsonb,1,'NC drawings use a reference origin.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.K',4,'Dimensioning for CNC from a single datum, called ordinate or baseline dimensioning, avoids the buildup of:','["colors", "layers", "tolerance errors", "lines"]'::jsonb,2,'Baseline dimensioning limits accumulation.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.L',3,'A parts list on a drawing includes the item number, the description, and the:','["date of birth", "owner", "paint", "quantity"]'::jsonb,3,'Parts lists show quantity.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.L',4,'A material specification such as ASTM A36 on a drawing tells the manufacturer which:','["grade of steel to use", "color to paint", "shift to work", "layer to use"]'::jsonb,0,'Specifications identify material grade.','original'),
('127.400.d.7.M',3,'A geometric tolerance is shown in a:','["title block", "feature control frame", "legend", "parts list"]'::jsonb,1,'Feature control frames hold GD&T.','original')
